slash discord. It is a great discord. I highly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.720
highly recommend it. Let's go next to Will from&nbsp;
L.A. now. Is that Los Angeles or Louisiana?&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.680
Speaker 3: It's Los Angeles.
Speaker 1: All right, welcome.&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.400
Speaker 3: How are you doing so I'm sorry if&nbsp;
this has already been brought up before, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.280
curious about your thoughts on this Gabby Petito&nbsp;
thing, obviously it's a huge story. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.800
on one side, you hear a lot of people kind of&nbsp;
irritated that a lot of black and indigenous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.880
and Hispanic people aren't getting the&nbsp;
same sort of visibility for their case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.320
I'm just curious what your thoughts on&nbsp;
that are. I mean, I personally think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.360
really tough to to demean this poor girl's&nbsp;
existence, to demean her own individual case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.400
by complaining about maybe other cases that&nbsp;
don't get the same sort of coverage. And I'm just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.320
curious what your what your thoughts on that are.
Speaker 1: Yes. So there's really three stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.760
here. And we talked about this on a&nbsp;
bonus show earlier this week. So first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.880
there's just the facts of the Gabby Petito story.&nbsp;
As of this moment, her her fiance, Brian Landry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.920
is missing. There's speculation as to his&nbsp;
involvement in her disappearance and subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.560
death, et cetera, et cetera. So there's the facts&nbsp;
of that story and the way that it's been reported.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.200
These stories, they do really well, particularly&nbsp;
for local news stations, like I think there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.920
station in Tampa that has just done really well&nbsp;
covering every aspect of this. And so it's sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.720
of like a car chase. Fox News starts covering&nbsp;
the car chase, et cetera. So there's that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.960
the salacious nature of the initial story. Then&nbsp;
you started to see commentaries from like Joy Read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.000
on MSNBC and some other places saying, hey, you&nbsp;
know what, this is missing white girl syndrome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.760
If this was a black woman, if this was an&nbsp;
indigenous woman or whatever, you would not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.360
seeing this amount of coverage. Newsweek even had&nbsp;
an article where they said, hey, you know what?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.360
Gabby Petito went missing in Wyoming over the last&nbsp;
decade. Four hundred sixty six indigenous women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.680
have gone missing in Wyoming. None of them got&nbsp;
this amount of coverage. I think to some degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.320
that's true. I also think it's a combination&nbsp;
of factors. It's pretty 22 year old white girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:35.280
some social media notoriety, a 90 minute or&nbsp;
hour long police stop body cam video that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.800
adds to the mystique and it adds to the story the&nbsp;
fiancee goes missing. You know, I think that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.680
are more aspects to it, but I think to some degree&nbsp;
there is a difference in coverage based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.960
identity of the of the victim. And then the third&nbsp;
story is many of the media outlets criticizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.640
the coverage, saying when it's a black woman or&nbsp;
an indigenous woman, we don't get the coverage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.960
They also never covered the missing black or&nbsp;
indigenous women, which is like the third layer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.880
which is they are making headlines, talking about&nbsp;
the disparity, but they contributed to that very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.800
same disparity. You know what I'm saying?
Speaker 3: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.720
I totally agree with that point. Absolutely.
Speaker 1: So in terms of not demeaning this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:23.600
what happened to Gabby Petito? I'm with you.&nbsp;
Like her case is no less tragic because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.200
these other media stories, that it's sort of like&nbsp;
running through a cancer fundraiser and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.880
what about AIDS? Right. Like, I get that it's&nbsp;
still important to understand why certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.000
stories get more coverage than others, though.
Speaker 3: Absolutely. It really is important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.040
understand and and I feel for for anybody that&nbsp;
